{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And it went up.</b> The meaning is that it was cut off, like 'When I rested my couch, it went up' (Genesis 49:4) — for the meaning is: from the time I rested, my couch withdrew from me and was cut off. And like it: 'He said to me: Do not go up in the midst of my days' (Psalms 102:24). And the dew would descend first to purify the earth, and when the dew withdrew, the manna descended. And so it is written: 'And when the dew fell upon the camp at night, the manna fell upon it' (Numbers 11:9)." "<b>דק מחוספס.</b> Like עגול, and it has no [elided] letter. And one who says it is like מחשוף (Genesis 30:37), which is with shin, is not correct, for the rule of the language is that they double the pe and the ayin, like יפיפית (Psalms 45:3), or the ayin and the lamed, like ירקרק (Leviticus 13:49), or the lamed alone, like סגריר (Proverbs 27:15). But the ayin alone we do not find doubled, except with the loss of a heh, like הגיגי (Psalms 5:2) — for after the heh dropped, the gimel appears as the lamed of the pattern פעל, therefore they doubled it. Only in visible letters we do not find the ayin doubled."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {1 "The word מחוספס poses a grammatical question: whether the samekh-pe is a reduplication pattern. Ibn Ezra argues it is not, since only certain letters get doubled in such patterns."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:08.824435799Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}